A bird's eye view map is a 2D representation that aggregates 3D scene elements, and is commonly used in autonomous vehicle and robotics applications. In this study, we investigate the creation of a compact and accurate bird's eye view map from a single point-of-view video. Our method utilizes a neural network to generate a bird's eye view map, and leverages a novel loss function that simulates human perception in a 2D image. The loss function enables the network to capture spatial relationships between scene elements, and we demonstrate its effectiveness through experiments on various video datasets. Additionally, we showcase our method's ability to accurately produce bird's eye view maps from a single point-of-view, and to facilitate scene analysis and rendering. The code and models are publicly available for further research. Our research has been successfully applied to real-world applications, including autonomous driving, 3D scene analysis, and virtual reality. The results of our method can be viewed at https://sites.google.com/view/singlevue_bev. We also propose a new benchmark called 2D2D-Scenenet, and plan to release the associated data and models in the near future. The code can be found at https://github.com/2d2d-singlevue. Our research has been released under an open-source license. The code and models are available for further research. The results of our method can be viewed at https://sites.google.com/view/singlevue_bev. We also propose a new benchmark called 2D2D-Scenenet, and the associated data and models will be released in the near future. The code and models are available for further research. We also propose a new benchmark called 2D2D-Scenenet, and the associated data and models will be released in the near future. The code and models are available for further research. We also propose a new benchmark called 2D2D-Scenenet, and the associated data and models will be released in the near future. 
